To solve the given problem, we'll use the properties of the cross product of vectors. The cross product of two vectors \(\mathbf{u}\) and \(\mathbf{v}\) is defined as:

\[
\mathbf{u} \times \mathbf{v} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
u_1 & u_2 & u_3 \\
v_1 & v_2 & v_3
\end{vmatrix}
= \left( u_2 v_3 - u_3 v_2 \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( u_1 v_3 - u_3 v_1 \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( u_1 v_2 - u_2 v_1 \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

Given:
\[
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -7 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix}
\]

We need to compute \((2 \mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}) \times (3 \mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a})\).

First, let's express \(2\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}\) and \(3\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}\):
\[
2\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a} = \mathbf{b} + \mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}
\]
\[
3\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}
\]

Next, we apply the distributive property of the cross product:
\[
(2\mathbf{b} - \mathbf{a}) \times (3\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}) = (2\mathbf{b} \times 3\mathbf{c}) + (2\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a}) - (\mathbf{a} \times 3\mathbf{c}) - (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{a})
\]

We already know:
\[
\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix}
\]
\[
2(\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c}) = 2 \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -14 \\ 36 \end{pmatrix}
\]

\[
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}
\]
\[
3(\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}) = 3 \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 12 \\ 21 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}
\]

\[
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{a} = 0 \quad \text{(since the cross product of a vector with itself is the zero vector)}
\]

Now compute the remaining cross products:
\[
2\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a} \quad \text{and} \quad \mathbf{a} \times 3\mathbf{c}
\]

We don't have explicit vectors \(\mathbf{a}\), \(\mathbf{b}\), and \(\mathbf{c}\), but we can use the given cross products to deduce these components indirectly.

Given:
\[
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -7 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}
\]
Let \(\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\ a_2 \\ a_3 \end{pmatrix}\) and \(\mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix}\).

From \(\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -7 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}\), we get:
\[
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\
b_1 & b_2 & b_3
\end{vmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
a_2 b_3 - a_3 b_2 \\
a_3 b_1 - a_1 b_3 \\
a_1 b_2 - a_2 b_1
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -7 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}
\]

Similarly, for \(\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}\):
\[
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\
c_1 & c_2 & c_3
\end{vmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
a_2 c_3 - a_3 c_2 \\
a_3 c_1 - a_1 c_3 \\
a_1 c_2 - a_2 c_1
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}
\]

For \(\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix}\):
\[
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
b_1 & b_2 & b_3 \\
c_1 & c_2 & c_3
\end{vmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}
b_2 c_3 - b_3 c_2 \\
b_3 c_1 - b_1 c_3 \\
b_1 c_2 - b_2 c_1
\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -7 \\ 18 \end{pmatrix}
\]

Using these, we can now compute \(2\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a}\) and \(\mathbf{a} \times 3\mathbf{c}\):

\[
2\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a} = 2 \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
b_1 & b_2 & b_3 \\
a_1 & a_2 & a_3
\end{vmatrix}
= 2 \begin{pmatrix}
b_2 a_3 - b_3 a_2 \\
b_3 a_1 - b_1 a_3 \\
b_1 a_2 - b_2 a_1
\end{pmatrix}
= 2 \begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -7 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 12 \\ -14 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}
\]

\[
\mathbf{a} \times 3\mathbf{c} = 3 \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\
c_1 & c_2 & c_3
\end{vmatrix}
= 3 \begin{pmatrix}
a_2 c_3 - a_3 c_2 \\
a_3 c_1 - a_1 c_3 \\
a_1 c_2 - a_2 c_1
\end{pmatrix}
= 3 \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 12 \\ 21 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}
\]

Combining all parts:
\[
(2\mathbf{b} \times 3\mathbf{c}) + (2\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{a}) - (\mathbf{a} \times 3\mathbf{c}) - (\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{a})
\]

\[
= \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -14 \\ 36 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 12 \\ -14 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 12 \\ 21 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
\]

\[
= \begin{pmatrix} 2 + 12 - 12 \\ -14 - 14 + 21 \\ 36 + 6 - 6 \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -7 \\ 36 \end{pmatrix}
\]

Thus, the final answer is:
\[
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -7 \\ 36 \end{pmatrix}}
\]

